{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>First passage</b><br>רפידתו — its couch-bedding, like ירפד חרוץ עלי מים." "רצוף — some say it is from רצפת אבנים (stone pavement), but it seems to me correct that it is a noun-form from ובידו רצפה." "<b>Second passage</b><br>Upon waking she went to seek her beloved, and [Scripture] says: Who is this that comes up from the wilderness like pillars of smoke, perfumed? Behold, his bed — [it is] complete: how could she walk alone? And behold, King Solomon needed many mighty men to guard his beloved, out of fear of raiders lest they seize her by night. He also needed to make a palanquin, and all this for the sake of one of the daughters of Jerusalem. And he proclaimed to make for him a great [procession], and he placed a crown of kingship on his head, so that he would be honored in the eyes of his beloved. And my beloved is more beautiful than it." "<b>Third passage</b><br>מרכבו ארגמן — like (purple) in the eye [i.e., in appearance]." "תוכו — the priests and the Levites."], :book :song-of-songs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {2 "Resolved אפיריון as 'palanquin' following its usage in Song 3:9; the word is a Greek loanword in Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra's commentary on the Canticles" "http://beta.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002030344/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:44:12.843765234Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}